id: k8s.port-forward
kind: kubernetes
title: port-forward
aliases: kubectl port-forward, tunnel
tags: operations, diagnostics
questions:
  - how do you reach a cluster service from your laptop?
invariant: port-forward opens a tunnel through the API server to one Pod (or one Service), bypassing Services and Ingress.
answer_30s: `kubectl port-forward` tunnels a local port to a Pod port through the API server, so you can test a single replica directly.
answer_2m: |
  Because it targets one Pod, it is the correct tool for "is this specific
  replica broken?" and the wrong tool for "is the Service broken?" -- by
  construction it skips the Service, endpoints, and ingress path, so a
  successful port-forward tells you the process is fine and nothing about
  routing. It is also the standard way to reach a database or admin port that
  is deliberately not exposed. Long-lived tunnels are for debugging, not
  access: they die with your terminal and authenticate with your RBAC, not the
  service's.
when_to_use:
  - reaching a specific Pod, a debug port, or a stateful service's admin interface
commands:
  - kubectl port-forward svc/api 8080:80 => local 8080 -> service (picks one backing Pod)
  - kubectl port-forward pod/api-7d9f-abc 5432:5432 => straight to one Pod, bypassing the Service
failure_modes:
  - concluding the Service works because port-forward worked (it bypasses it)
related:
  - k8s.service
  - k8s.exec
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_port-forward/
